<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · c. 100 · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Seikilos epitaph</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The oldest complete song we can still read, music and all, carved on a stone pillar in Turkey almost 2,000 years ago.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>not known (the stone names Seikilos as the one who set it up)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Greek-speaking people of Roman Asia Minor</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Tralles (modern Aydın), Turkey</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1st or 2nd century AD</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words anonymous or by Seikilos (inscription of the 1st or 2nd century AD))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seikilos-epitaph">Seikilos epitaph</a> is a Greek inscription on a marble pillar (stele) from Tralles in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, now the city of Aydın in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It preserves the oldest surviving complete musical composition: older notated music exists, such as the Hurrian songs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delphic-hymns">Delphic Hymns</a>, but only in fragments. The stone carries an elegiac couplet in which the stone itself speaks to the passer-by, as in many ancient epitaphs, and below it a short song with signs for the melody written above the words. It is commonly dated to the 1st or 2nd century AD; scholars reading the letter forms have argued firmly for each of the two centuries.</p><p>Its last line, now lost, named Seikilos and, in the usual reconstruction, Euterpe, with a verb meaning &quot;is alive&quot; that ancient dedicators used when they had outlived the person they commemorated. On this reading Seikilos set up the stone for a woman named Euterpe, perhaps his wife; others read the name as the Muse of lyric poetry and music, or as Seikilos's father. Armand D'Angour has proposed that the pillar was not a tomb marker at all but a monument Seikilos raised to his own skill as a poet and musician.</p><p>The stele was found around 1883 by the Irish engineer Edward Purser while the Ottoman Railway was being built at Aydın, and the archaeologist William Mitchell Ramsay published a description that year. A rubbing made before 1893 was published by Théodore Reinach in 1894. Purser had the damaged base sawn flat so that his wife could stand flowerpots on it, which destroyed the last line; it is known only from the rubbing. The pillar passed to his son-in-law in Buca. During the Burning of Smyrna in 1922, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> consul in İzmir took it, and it travelled by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, where it stayed, presumed lost, until 1966. The National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> then acquired it, revealed it the following year and still shows it.</p><p>The song is diatonic. Thomas J. Mathiesen and Jon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> place it in the Iastian tonos, while noting that its arrangement of tones is that of the Phrygian species; others describe it as a segment of the Ionian scale. In 1893 Otto Crusius observed that its melody, like the hymns of Mesomedes, tends to follow the pitch accents of the Greek words, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delphic-hymns">Delphic Hymns</a>, published the same year, confirmed. Dots above some notes, the stigmai, are still debated: they may mark the raised half of each bar or a rhythmic stress, and D'Angour, Stefan Hagel and Tosca Lynch disagree on how the song should be barred. The melody is also strikingly close to the Hosanna antiphon of the Roman liturgy for Palm Sunday.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A short song about making the most of life: while you live, be seen and do not grieve, because life is brief and time takes its due in the end. Set on a memorial stone, it turns a reminder of death into advice to the living.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Ancient Greek</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Ancient Greek</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">ὅσον ζῇς, φαίνου</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">hoson zēis, phainou</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As long as you live, shine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">μηδὲν ὅλως σὺ λυποῦ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">mēden holōs sy lypou</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>do not grieve at all at anything;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">πρὸς ὀλίγον ἔστι τὸ ζῆν</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">pros oligon esti to zēn</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>life is for a short while,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">τὸ τέλος ὁ χρόνος ἀπαιτεῖ.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">to telos ho chronos apaitei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>time demands its end.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: The Seikilos stele (1st or 2nd century CE); the Greek text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seikilos_epitaph" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (classical transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'phainou' is 'appear, show yourself, shine': be seen, be bright while you live.</li><li>line 1.4: literally 'time demands back the end' (apaitei: to demand what is owed): time claims its due. 'To telos' can be the end or the fulfilment.</li><li>Romanization: accents left out, rough breathing as h, iota subscript written as i (zēis), eta and omega with macrons, upsilon as y.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
